@node Runner Reference
@chapter Runner Reference

This chapter is the complete command-line reference for @command{agentic-run}.
All commands operate on the registry that is supplied to
@code{Agentic.Cli.cliMain}.  The noun below is @samp{example} in this
repository.

@include runner-index.texi

@section Synopsis

@example
agentic-run list [--json]
agentic-run --routing [--json] [--persona NAME] [--offline | --refresh-models]
agentic-run --migrate-routing SOURCE --output DESTINATION
agentic-run help NAME
agentic-run NAME --help
agentic-run plan NAME [--raw] [--require-pinned] [--json] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run cost NAME [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --scripted [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --session ID [DECK-OPTION ...] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run run NAME --engine acp [ACP-OPTION ...] [ROUTING-OPTION ...] [INPUT ...]
agentic-run machine RUN_ID NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run lineage-check restart|resume|fork PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-restart RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-resume RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME RUN-OPTION ...
agentic-run machine-fork RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME [FORK-EDIT ...] RUN-OPTION ...
@end example

A verb in head position wins over a registered row of the same name.  Help has
two per-row spellings and one general spelling.  A bare registered name is
refused rather than interpreted as help or execution.

@section Verbs

@table @code
@c COVER CLI verb:list author
@item list [--json]
Print every registered name and its one-line description in registry order.
JSON form emits an array of static fact objects.  No Program is executed.

@c COVER CLI verb:help author
@item help [NAME]
With no name, print general usage.  With one name, build the row at empty inputs
and print its computed facts and help page.  @code{NAME --help} is byte-identical.

@c COVER CLI verb:plan author
@item plan NAME [options]
Build the Program and print its static level, size, ask nodes, code sequence
when one exists, and cost summary.  @code{--raw} adds the emitted first-order
Program, and @code{--json} selects the machine object.

@c COVER CLI verb:cost author
@item cost NAME [inputs]
Print the cost summary and every folded syntactic path.  Machine-readable cost
data is available through @code{plan --json}, and @code{cost} accepts no JSON
option.

@c COVER CLI verb:run author
@item run NAME target [options]
Build, preflight, and execute the Program against exactly one scripted target or
a live route table with one default backend.  The trace is printed as the run
progresses, followed by result and bills.

@c COVER CLI verb:machine author
@item machine RUN_ID NAME target [options]
Execute the same Program through the durable runner, and emit strict version-1
NDJSON events on standard output.  Workflow text uses standard input.
Correlated controls use the inherited descriptor that
@code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_FD} names, which is descriptor 3 in Pi.  The private
store and the owner metadata use the remaining documented @code{AGENT_CAT_*}
environment variables.  Human prompt narration is omitted from machine
diagnostics.  @code{manifest.json} references a separate @code{program.json}
with mode 0600.  This keeps input-expanded text out of the manifest and
preserves exact lineage validation.

@c COVER CLI verb:lineage-check author
@item lineage-check OPERATION PARENT_STORE NAME target [options]
Validate restart, resume, or fork compatibility without a child run.  Resume
requires a compatible checkpoint, and restart and fork do not.

@c COVER CLI verb:machine-restart author
@c COVER CLI verb:machine-resume author
@c COVER CLI verb:machine-fork author
@item machine-restart|machine-resume|machine-fork RUN_ID PARENT_STORE NAME target [options]
Create a durable child run with an immutable parent link.  Restart inherits no
answers.  Resume uses an exact compatible checkpoint.  Fork validates any
explicit edits and then reuses the matching complete answers.

@end table

@section Top-level routing modes

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --routing author
@item --routing [--json] [--persona NAME] [--offline | --refresh-models]
Load the conventional routing files and print the resolved policy without an
engine or a run store.  Human output names the persona, axes, aliases, exact model
identifiers, engines, and inventory source.  JSON also carries the available
persona and model choices, credential-readiness booleans, profile rungs, cache
ages and timestamps, warnings, and endpoint fingerprints.  It omits secret names
and values, environment and header data, raw endpoint URLs, and unselected
inventories.

@c COVER CLI --migrate-routing author
@c COVER CLI --output author
@item --migrate-routing SOURCE --output DESTINATION
Decode one complete schema-version-1 file and create an offline version-2 user
file exclusively.  The output has one engine per router, one exact model alias
per router and model pair, one @code{default} persona, and equivalent profile
chains.  The source and every existing destination are refused rather than
overwritten.
@end table


@section Common inspection options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --help author
@item --help
Alone, print usage at exit 0.  After a row name, print that row's page.  It is
not accepted as an option to other verb forms.

@c COVER CLI --json author
@item --json
Machine rendering for @code{list}, @code{plan}, and @code{--routing}.  With
@code{plan --raw}, it adds the @code{program} key.  Help, cost, and run have no
JSON form, and refusals remain prose on standard error.

@c COVER CLI --raw author
@item --raw
With @code{plan}, print or include the emitted @code{RawProgram}.  It can be
combined with @code{--json} and with input flags in any order.

@c COVER CLI --require-pinned author
@item --require-pinned
With @code{plan} or @code{run}, refuse unless every model question names a
primary serving model.  Checked before output, process startup, or spending.
@end table

@section Input options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --input author
@item --input FILE
Supply the sole operator input of a Program that declares exactly one.  The path
is not parsed as @samp{NAME=FILE}, so an equals sign remains part of the path.

@c COVER CLI --input-file author
@item --input-file NAME=FILE
Supply a named operator input from UTF-8 file contents.  It can repeat.
Invalid UTF-8 is refused.  An ordinary prompt input strips one trailing
newline.  Declared command-tail and stdin inputs preserve the exact decoded
text.

@c COVER CLI --input-arg author
@item --input-arg NAME=VALUE
Supply a named operator input inline.  It can repeat.  The value can contain
equals signs, because the parse splits at the first one.
@end table

Plan, cost, and run accept the input flags.  A Program with no operator inputs
refuses them.  A valid @code{run.} fact name is also refused, because the runner
supplies it.  An unknown reserved name is an authoring error.  During
@code{run}, machine, and machine-lineage commands, an unbound stdin declaration
reads descriptor 0 to end of file.  Plan and cost never read stdin
automatically.

Machine supervisors set @code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_FD} to an inherited descriptor
of at least 3, and Pi uses descriptor 3.  Control NDJSON and workflow input
therefore coexist without framing.  The compatibility form
@code{AGENT_CAT_CONTROL_STDIN=1} puts controls on standard input instead.  It
works only when the stdin-designated value was supplied explicitly, and both
control modes together are refused.

@section Target and transport options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --scripted author
@item --scripted
Select the registered canned table.  It is mutually exclusive with
@code{--engine}, @code{--session}, every live transport option, @code{--route},
and every version-2 persona or discovery override.

@c COVER CLI --engine author
@item --engine acp
Select an ACP default backend.  @code{--engine deck} is accepted only with
@code{--session ID}, and a bare session already selects deck.  Any other word
is refused.

@c COVER CLI --session author
@item --session ID
Select an agent-deck session as the default backend.  Mutually exclusive with
scripted and ACP defaults.

@c COVER CLI --adapter author
@item --adapter stub|claude|codex|droid|PATH
Select the ACP adapter.  It defaults to @code{stub} and is meaningful only when
at least one ACP backend exists.

@c COVER CLI --adapter-arg author
@item --adapter-arg ARG
Append an argument to every ACP adapter argv.  Repeatable and meaningful only
for a run that reaches ACP.

@c COVER CLI --scratch author
@item --scratch DIR
Set the working and authorized effect directory for ACP backends.  It defaults
to a fresh temporary directory printed in the run header and is meaningful only
when ACP is used.

@c COVER CLI --binary author
@item --binary PATH
Set the @command{agent-deck} executable for deck backends.  It defaults to
@code{agent-deck} on @code{PATH}.

@c COVER CLI --poll author
@item --poll MS
Set the interval between deck status checks.  The default interval is 1000
milliseconds, and the value must be a nonnegative integer.

@c COVER CLI --timeout author
@item --timeout MS
Set the per-request or per-turn budget for every live backend.  Without the
flag, ACP uses 900000 milliseconds and deck uses 600000.  The CLI accepts a
nonnegative integer.  Zero leaves ACP unbounded but gives deck a deadline that
has already expired, so a mixed run must use a positive value.

@c COVER CLI --verbose author
@item --verbose
Narrate live transport commands, polls, and protocol activity on standard error.
It is refused under scripted execution.
@end table

@section Fork edit options

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --drop-answer author
@item --drop-answer OCCURRENCE_ID
For @code{lineage-check fork} or @code{machine-fork}, omit one persisted answer
from inheritance so that occurrence runs again.  Repeatable.

@c COVER CLI --set-answer author
@item --set-answer OCCURRENCE_ID=JSON_FILE
Replace one inherited answer with the single JSON value in @code{JSON_FILE}.
The runner validates it against the persisted answer code or schema before child
creation.  Repeatable and valid only for fork.
@end table

@section Routing

@table @code
@c COVER CLI --route author
@item --route NAME=BACKEND
Route one serving-model pin to @code{acp:ADAPTER} or @code{deck:SESSION}.  It can
repeat at most once per name.  The Program must contain the named primary or
alternate pin, and a default live backend is still required.  Schema version 1
and unconfigured version-2 names retain this behavior.  A managed version-2 axis
refuses raw backend substitution.

@c COVER CLI --persona author
@item --persona NAME
Select a version-2 persona explicitly.  Precedence is this option,
@code{AGENT_CAT_PERSONA}, the project selector, and then the user default.  No
repository, branch, hostname, account, or email address is inferred.

@c COVER CLI --realize author
@item --realize AXIS=MODEL-ALIAS
Replace one managed runtime axis with a concrete alias that the selected persona
allows.  It can repeat once per axis.  The owning engine of the alias must also
appear in the engine allowlist of the persona.

@c COVER CLI --offline author
@item --offline
Perform no catalogue request.  A cache no older than @code{stale-if-error} can
satisfy an inventory-dependent selector, and an exact selector can remain
@code{static-unverified}.

@c COVER CLI --refresh-models author
@item --refresh-models
Force every required catalogue to refresh.  A failure is a setup refusal and
never falls back silently to stale cache.  This option is mutually exclusive
with @code{--offline}.
@end table

A route table can contain both schemes.  ACP-specific and deck-specific flags
are accepted when their corresponding scheme occurs anywhere in that table and
are refused otherwise.  Routes do not alter Plan folds or bills, because backend
identity is absent from the semantic key.

The version-2 user file is
@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/agent-cat/routing.yaml}, with @file{~/.config} as its
fallback.  The project layer is the nearest @file{.agent-cat/routing.yaml} up to
the Git boundary.  Discovery assigns these roles before decoding; document shape
cannot promote a project file to user authority, and untagged version-2 loading
is refused.  Mixed schema versions fail before a secret lookup.  The CLI
resolves environment references only for engines that remain after the routing
and run-fact fixed point.  ACP inherits the ambient environment after every
declared source and destination name is removed, and then receives the selected
bindings.  This is a routing context, not credential isolation from files,
profiles, or the operating system.

The bounded Haskell HTTP client fetches OpenAI and Anthropic catalogue responses,
and it never invokes @command{curl}.  It verifies authenticated TLS, limits a URL
to 8192 UTF-8 bytes and its query to 4096 bytes and 64 items, and rechecks those
bounds after it adds pagination parameters.  The complete explicit header set,
including resolved authentication and the generated request identifier, shares
the count, value, and aggregate-byte bounds.  Response bytes, pages, entries,
model identifiers, and time are also bounded.  Redirects are
disabled.  Atomic files with
mode 0600 are stored below
@file{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/agent-cat/models/PERSONA/FINGERPRINT.json}.  The path
fingerprint covers the complete non-secret engine definition, including cache
policy, while a separate endpoint fingerprint explains model selection.  A cache
record is bounded before allocation, and managed cache directories cannot be
symbolic links.  The exact selection, its inventory provenance, and the
engine-definition fingerprint enter the manifest policy with a canonical SHA-256
digest before the first question.  The complete snapshot remains immutable
provenance.  Restart and resume compare its stable execution projection, so an
observation-time inventory source, fetch time, cache age, warning, or the digest
derived from those fields cannot reject otherwise identical routing.  Persona
selection, engine and endpoint fingerprints, exact model, settings, options, and
routes remain significant.

@section Outputs and exit status

The human output of @code{plan}, @code{cost}, and @code{help} is descriptive,
and it is not a stable machine interface.  @code{list --json},
@code{plan --json}, and @code{--routing --json} use stable key names.  JSON
object key order is not significant, and the input array follows declaration
order.  @code{list --json} emits descriptor version 3 with routing and
protocol-negotiation capabilities.  The machine protocol and store format remain
at version 1.

Run output is the trace record as it happens.  There is no summary JSON,
because a second representation either omits or duplicates the event stream.
Standard error carries refusals, transport narration, failed attempts, and
recovery diagnostics.

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .88
@headitem Status @tab Meaning
@item 0 @tab Inspection completed or the run reached its represented end.
@item 1 @tab Usage or preflight refusal.  No live execution has started.
@item 2 @tab Transport failure, missing process, protocol error, stopped session,
or timeout.
@item 3 @tab Run abandonment after answer recovery or failed required completion
evidence.
@item 130 @tab Machine run cancelled by a control command or by control-input
end of file.
@end multitable

@node Diagnostics and Troubleshooting
@chapter Diagnostics and Troubleshooting

This chapter begins with common questions.  It then relates compiler and
runner symptoms to the layer that can explain them.  Handle a diagnostic from
Haskell, from Program preflight, from typed answer recovery, or from transport
execution at that same layer.

@section Where to look

@table @asis
@item What is agent-cat for?
Read @ref{Introduction}.  The architecture and evidence limits follow in
@ref{Architecture and Guarantees}.
@item Why can a repeated question be asked once yet appear twice in a trace?
Read @ref{Memoization and event identity}.  Semantic occurrences and operational
answer reuse are distinct.
@item Why can the displayed minimum cost be unattainable?
Read @ref{Cost trees and bills}.  The tree combines syntactic arms, while
worlds can correlate separate choices.
@item How is a workflow run without credentials?
Use scripted execution in @ref{Scripted execution}.
@item Which Haskell word or type is public?
Look it up in @ref{Haskell Authoring Reference} or the Haskell API index.
@item Which command-line flag belongs to an engine?
Use @ref{Runner Reference} or the CLI index.
@item What does a mathematical term mean here?
Use @ref{Glossary}.  Each cross-cutting term has one canonical definition
there.
@end table

@section Haskell authoring diagnostics

@table @samp
@item Variable not in scope
A prompt hole or later expression names no Haskell variable in lexical scope.
Correct the spelling or move the use into the block or case arm where its handle
is bound.  The workflow layer performs no second string-based lookup.

@item this binding is not live here
A typed handle has crossed its scope boundary, commonly from one branch arm into
another or outside a function body.  Pass a supported value through a function
argument or place the use within the handle's lexical scope.

@item a hole in a [wft|...|] prompt names a define
A @code{wft} fence has no live workflow scope.  Use @code{wf} in the block where
the handle is live, or convert only author-supplied define text through
@code{wft}.

@item Couldn't match 'CodeVerdict with 'CodeText
A source, parameter, argument, branch, or function result has the wrong answer
kind.  Follow the two types that GHC names.  Select a question kind with
@code{confirm}, @code{answering}, @code{annotated}, or @code{structured}, and
keep function parameters and call arguments exact.

@item nothing follows a terminal
A statement appears after @code{stop}, @code{ask_}, @code{if}, a revision case,
@code{when}, or @code{unless}.  Move the statement into every applicable arm or
place it before the terminal.

@item Couldn't match Open with Body
A function-body terminal was placed in a workflow, or a workflow terminal was
placed in a function.  Use @code{answer} or @code{done} to return from a function
and @code{stop} or another workflow terminal to finish the main block.

@item a function body is a straight line
A branch, bounded revision, or @code{knownHere} was placed inside a function
body.  Move finite control to the caller and call the straight-line function
from each arm.

@item a bounded revision reviews first ... and then amends
A revision clause contains an extra statement, lacks its verdict review, or does
not finish with @code{amend}.  Retain exactly the two represented clause sources.

@item panel: a panel needs at least one member
The authoring list is empty.  Supply at least one question.  For @code{panelText},
also give every member a valid distinct label.

@item decide: a decider needs at least one needle
The classifier has no meaningful literal test.  Supply one or more nonempty
needles.  A test that is chosen from model-authored text changes the intended
trust boundary, and it is unsupported.

@item named: b0 is a name this surface generates for itself
A selected binding or parameter uses the reserved @code{bN} or @code{rN} shape.
Choose a descriptive name outside those families.

@item function declared twice or call names a function not declared earlier
The @code{defining} table has a duplicate, forward call, or omitted callee.  Put
each function once and order dependencies before callers.  Recursive tables are
outside the finite authoring language.
@end table

@section Program and command-line refusals

@table @samp
@item no verb 'NAME'
The first word is neither a CLI verb nor an accepted help form.  Use
@code{list}, @code{help}, @code{plan}, @code{cost}, or @code{run}.  A registered
name by itself is deliberately ambiguous and is refused.

@item no example named 'NAME'
The registry has no row of that name.  Run @code{agentic-run list} and use its
exact spelling.

@item has no input named 'NAME'
An input flag names no operator input declared by the Program.  Read the
@code{inputs} line in help, plan, or list JSON.  Run facts appear separately and
cannot be supplied by a flag.

@item input 'run.engine' is a run fact
The runner owns that value.  Remove the input flag, because a live or scripted
run binds the fact automatically.

@item --input needs a program with exactly one input
Use @code{--input-file NAME=FILE} or @code{--input-arg NAME=VALUE} when several
operator inputs exist.  A Program with none accepts no input flag.

@item program asks an unpinned model question
@code{--require-pinned} found a model party without @code{servedBy}.  Add a
primary serving model in source or run without the opt-in guard when unpinned
dispatch is intended.

@item --route names a model this program never pins
The left side must be a primary or alternate serving-model pin from the Program,
rather than the party identifier.  Inspect @code{pins} through help or JSON.

@item --route refines this run's default answerer, and there is none
Add @code{--engine acp} or @code{--session ID}.  A route table sends only named
pins elsewhere, and unclaimed questions still require a default.

@item FLAG is not the ENGINE engine's to take
The option is inert, because the selected backend table contains no matching
scheme.  Remove it, or add an actual ACP or deck backend that consumes it.

@item run needs --scripted, --engine acp, or --session ID
Select exactly one default answering service.  Mixed live runs still begin with
one default and add routes.
@end table

@section Execution failures

@table @asis
@item Exit 2 with @samp{could not run}
The adapter, the deck binary, or another required process was not found or did
not start.  Confirm the path and the execute permission before you examine
prompts.

@item Exit 2 with protocol or EOF wording
The live transport ended, or it spoke a message outside the expected protocol.
Use @code{--verbose} and the named failing method.  The represented Program
has not supplied a typed answer for this failure.

@item Exit 2 with @samp{did not answer within ...ms}
The transport exceeded its turn budget.  Check whether the session is stopped,
stale, or still working before increasing @code{--timeout}.  A larger budget
does not repair a session that cannot produce newer output.

@item Exit 3 after an unreadable answer
The normal decoder and the configured re-asks did not obtain a value of the
required kind.  Read the quoted bytes and the answer specification.  Correct
the prompt or the answering service, and do not add a default answer that was
never given.

@item Exit 3 after a cancelled ACP effect
ACP reported a turn that lacked required completion evidence.  The runner
refuses to record a receipt for that occurrence.  Inspect permission and stop
reason before retrying.

@item billMemo is smaller than billFresh
Two reached consult or observe occurrences shared a bare-question memo key.
This is expected reuse.  Effects remain per occurrence.  Compare the annotated
trace when the difference is surprising.

@item billFresh is outside the plan range
Either the run did not execute the Plan that was priced, or a defect exists in
reporting or conformance.  Record @code{plan --raw}, the inputs, the run
facts, and the complete trace.  Every accepted branch-level Program has a sound
interval theorem.

@item generated binder names differ from the source
The authoring library prints depth names, because it cannot inspect the
spelling of a Haskell binder.  Use @code{named} when human-readable first-order
output matters.  Scope-aware alpha comparison still checks binding identity in
the curated cases.
@end table

@section Conformance and build failures

@table @asis
@item @command{tier0} reports a corpus mismatch
The Haskell codec, the guards, the string layer, or the observation diverged
from a committed Lean reply.  Treat a corpus request as specification data.
Regenerate only when you change that specification deliberately.

@item @command{tier1} cannot find the conformance oracle
Build the Lean @command{conformance-oracle} once outside the script, then rerun.
The gate refuses to build or silently skip it and must not overlap another Lean
build.

@item corpus regeneration changes files
The current Lean oracle does not produce the committed observations.  Review
the diff as a specification change.  A successful regeneration changes nothing
and leaves @code{git status --short bisim/corpus} empty.

@item Texinfo check reports missing coverage
Run @code{python3 doc/check-manual-coverage.py --list} to see the
source-derived inventory.  Add or classify the new export or CLI item in the
reference, and update its exact index entry.  Keep the checker policy aligned
with the intended public status.
@end table

@node Glossary
@chapter Glossary

This glossary gives one canonical meaning for the terms used across the manual.
Definitions point outward to detailed treatment rather than repeating it.

@table @dfn
@cindex addressee
@item addressee
The authored model, tool, executable tool, or person to whom a question is put.
It is part of semantic question identity.

@cindex answer code
@item answer code
A member of @code{Code} describing the type of an answer: text, verdict, flag,
receipt, or a structured schema.

@cindex answer source
@item answer source
Execution annotation stating that an answer was reused or was asked from a
particular dispatched question.

@cindex annotated execution trace
@item annotated execution trace
The Plan-ordered list of execution events retaining authored intent and answer
source.  Erasure yields the semantic trace.

@cindex ask node
@item ask node
A request occurrence that is written in the Plan.  The static @code{askNodes}
count covers all branch arms, and a run reaches only one selected path.

@cindex bare question
@item bare question
The typed @code{Q c} without execution intent.  It is the key for worlds,
semantic events, and reusable memo answers.

@cindex bill, fresh
@item billFresh
A price that is folded over the semantic transcript.  It counts every reached
request occurrence.

@cindex bill, memo
@item billMemo
The operational bill that deduplicates reusable bare questions and retains every
effect occurrence.

@cindex binding
@item binding
A typed answer made available to the remainder of a workflow block through a
Haskell handle and a Plan context entry.

@cindex branch rung
@item branch
The analysis rung at which finite cases are present and a finite cost multiset
contains every world's bill.

@cindex closed question
@item closed question
A request whose complete prompt is literal in the represented term.  It uses the
@code{askC} Plan form.

@cindex conformance
@item conformance
Versioned comparison between Haskell's emitted representation and observations
and the Lean oracle or its committed replies.  It establishes agreement only on
the compared fields.

@cindex consult intent
@item consult
Execution intent for an ordinary value question.  Its answer is reusable by bare
question identity.

@cindex context
@item context
The type-level list of answer codes live at a point in a Plan, written
@code{Γ}.

@cindex cost tree
@item cost tree
The finite multiset of syntactic path bills computed for a branch-level Plan
under a shape-based price.

@cindex decider
@item decider
A member of the closed pure classification vocabulary that turns bound text into
a flag without asking an addressee.

@cindex denotation
@item denotation
The meaning function from a typed Plan and environment to a dialogue.  It erases
execution intent.

@cindex dialogue
@item dialogue
A well-founded typed question-and-continuation tree.  A run in a world
produces a result, and a trace produces semantic events.

@cindex dispatched question
@item dispatched question
The operationally relabeled question actually sent after routing or fail-over.
The authored question remains in the execution event.

@cindex draw
@item draw
The question field used for deliberate resampling.  Distinct draws form distinct
questions and memo keys.

@cindex effect intent
@item effect
Occurrence-sensitive execution intent for a statement-position act.  It uses
the receipt code, bypasses reusable memo state, reserves the effect lane, and
can request permission.

@cindex environment
@item environment
A typed product @code{Env Γ} containing one answer value for each code in the
Plan context.

@cindex evidence ceiling
@item evidence ceiling
The strongest conclusion licensed by a proof, trace, conformance record, or
transport report.  Physical effects and faithful external observations require
operational evidence beyond denotation.

@cindex first-order representation
@item first-order
A representation made only of data constructors and values, with no Haskell or
Lean closures.  @code{RawProgram} is first-order.

@cindex fresh bill
@item fresh bill
See @code{billFresh}.

@cindex function, workflow
@item function
A named straight-line open Plan over typed parameters.  Calls substitute
arguments and graft the body rather than adding runtime dispatch.

@cindex intent
@item intent
The consult, observe, or effect annotation on one Plan request occurrence.  It
guides execution and is erased by denotation.

@cindex memo table
@item memo table
The typed runtime table that is keyed by bare questions.  Consult and observe
use it, and effects bypass it.

@cindex observe intent
@item observe
Execution intent for an executable tool in value position.  Its answer is
reusable.  The tag alone does not establish that the argv is read-only.

@cindex open question
@item open question
A request whose prompt expression reads earlier answers while its shape remains
fixed.  It uses the @code{ask} Plan form and forces at least the pipeline rung.

@cindex panel
@item panel
A nonempty fan-out of questions folded in member order by the verdict algebra or
the named text-block algebra.

@cindex pipeline rung
@item pipeline
The analysis rung at which prompt words can depend on earlier answers while
question shapes and path structure stay fixed.

@cindex Plan
@item Plan
The intrinsically typed five-form workflow representation over a context and
result type.

@cindex Program
@item Program
The Haskell builder result pairing an emitted @code{RawProgram} with the typed
Haskell Plan constructed from the same authoring operations.

@cindex prompt
@item prompt
The authored words of a question.  A prompt expression can combine literals
and textual renderings of earlier answers.

@cindex question
@item question
A typed value @code{Q c} containing addressee, scope, prompt, and draw, and
requiring an answer in @code{El c}.

@cindex RawProgram
@item RawProgram
The first-order function table and main workflow emitted by Haskell and accepted
by the Lean checker on the conformance boundary.

@cindex receipt
@item receipt
The author-facing name for the unit acknowledgement code.  The first-order code
constructor and some wire positions use the name @code{ack}.

@cindex route
@item route
A live-run mapping from a serving-model pin to an ACP or agent-deck backend.
Routing is execution policy and does not alter Plan folds.

@cindex run fact
@item run fact
One of four input texts supplied by the runner about its resolved run: backends,
engine policy, routes, or sentinel.

@cindex schema
@item schema
The promoted algebra describing a structured answer as primitives, arrays, and
named product fields.

@cindex semantic event
@item semantic event
A dependent record of answer code, authored bare question, and semantic answer.

@cindex semantic trace
@item semantic trace
The ordered list of semantic events produced by dialogue trace or by erasing an
annotated execution trace.

@cindex serving model
@item serving model
The model pin that is authored on a model question.  The primary participates
in scope, and the alternates form a fail-over chain.

@cindex shape
@item shape
The part of a question known before prompt words are evaluated: addressee, scope,
and draw.  Shape-based prices support exact pipeline cost.

@cindex scope
@item scope
The authored policy axes that a question carries, among them the serving-model
axis.  The word is also used informally for the typed set of live bindings
where the context is clear.

@cindex structured answer
@item structured answer
An answer whose code carries a schema and whose semantic value is interpreted by
@code{SchemaEl}.  JSON is one validated boundary representation.

@cindex terminal
@item terminal
A workflow construction after which no statement follows in that block, such as
@code{stop}, @code{ask_}, or a complete branch.

@cindex verdict
@item verdict
The ordered algebra in which approval leaves another verdict unchanged, refusal
determines the result, and objection reason lists are concatenated.

@cindex world
@item world
A total typed answer sheet @code{Ω = (c : Code) -> Q c -> El c}.  It is the
mathematical environment in which a dialogue is observed.
@end table

